  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nHigh pressure to the north continues to feed cool/dry air into the \nregion. NAM BUFKIT profiles show mixed winds stalled for the \nremainder of the day, meaning winds have peaked. Fires remain a \nconcern given the antecedent dry conditions and low humidity, \nbut again the limiting factor is winds.\n\nRegarding smoke in the upper levels, overall little change with the \nHRRR/RAP continuing to pump smoke out of the Pacific Northwest, send \nit across the Northern Rockies into the Dakotas as the upper level \nflow regime changes little overall. There is a little less overhead \ncurrently, but more backs in late today. Concerning low level smoke, \nthere isn't much evidence for near surface smoke between ASOS/AWOS \nvisibility sensors and southwest ND/western SD webcams. HRRR/RAP \nonly bring low level smoke up to the border of the CWA for Saturday, \nsomething to watch. \n\nNext on the list is the convective potential for tonight/Saturday \nmorning. This is more a classic low level jet setup across \nwestern/central South Dakota with a 25-30kt southerly jet aimed at \ncentral/south central South Dakota, with westerly flow and warm \nadvection at 700mb. 700mb temperatures are pushing +15C in the post \nconvective airmass but out ahead its only about +9C. NAM BUFKIT \nprofiles show quite a bit of elevated instability (3k j/kg) embedded \nwithin unidirectional west-northwesterly flow of about 40kts, though \nCAMS are more reserved at 2-2.5k j/kg CAPE. There is only a handful \nof j/kg CIN in the NAM profiles as daytime heating kicks in just \nahead of convection. Thus, as storms are progressing east there \ncould be a window for surface based convection. Convection will have \nto walk a fine line as those capping temperatures rapidly spread \neast as well. Regardless, residence time in the CWA should be short \nas convection presses east at 40kts into southeast South Dakota. \nBroadened NBM POPs in time and coverage since for the most part they \nwere too low for a mention of weather, but stuck to just 20%.\n\nMaybe a northwest flow wave for Saturday night/Sunday morning as \nwell, briefly suppressing 700mb warm air aloft. Depiction of \nconvection in the 4km NAM and NSSL WRF associated with an inverted \ntrough into northeast South Dakota/southeast North Dakota. Even if \nnot surface based NAM BUFKIT profiles again present us with elevated \nCAPE and west northwesterly unidirectional flow which is closer to \n40-50kts. \n\nNBM is still pretty bullish for Sunday night/Monday morning in \nregards to POPs and the respective NBM probabilities for amounts \nhasn't changed much run-to-run, but looking at deterministic \nguidance there is much less of a signal for a northwest flow type \nwave at 500mb. There is still a plume of moisture pivoting around \nthe 4-Corners high from Idaho/Montana/Wyoming before ejecting into \nSouth Dakota. What you end up with is moisture above 600mb with a \nskinny elevated CAPE profile. Again, deterministic guidance has \ntrended down in QPF however, and the main driver appears to be EC \nensembles having more QPF in comparison to GEFS/Canadian, a trend \nthat starts with the EC deterministic being the main model with QPF \nof consequence.",
